::STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best fits one numerical variable such as daily reading minutes?
{
~A. Two-way table#A two-way table summarizes two categorical variables.
=B. Histogram#A histogram can show the distribution of one numerical variable.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart shows categorical parts of a whole.
~D. Segmented bar graph#Segmented bar graphs compare categorical proportions.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A scatterplot point represents one student with study time 4 hours and score 88. What does the point mean?
{
=A. One case has two numerical values#Each point in a scatterplot is a paired numerical observation.
~B. The class average is 88#One point is not the class average.
~C. Four students scored 88#The coordinate 4 is a value, not a count of students.
~D. The data are categorical#Study time and score are numerical.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table shows Grade 9: bus 50%, car 30%, walk 20%. What should a segmented bar show?
{
~A. Three bars with heights 50, 30, and 20 students#Those would be counts, not one segmented percent bar.
~B. A scatterplot with three points#The data are categorical proportions, not paired numerical values.
~C. A box plot with quartiles#Quartiles summarize numerical data, not transportation categories.
=D. A Grade 9 bar split into 50%, 30%, and 20% segments#Segment lengths match the relative frequencies and total 100%.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best fits two numerical variables such as practice time and free throw percentage?
{
=A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot shows a relationship between two numerical variables.
~B. Bar chart#A bar chart compares categories.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a categorical whole.
~D. Two-way table#A two-way table is for two categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Why is justification important when selecting a display?
{
~A. It replaces the need to read labels#Labels still need to be read.
~B. It proves causation automatically#Display choice does not prove causation.
=C. It explains how the display matches the data type, variables, and question#A justification connects the display to the statistical purpose.
~D. It makes every display equally correct#Some displays fit the data better than others.
}
